  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 354 AM CDT Mon Jun 1 2026\n\nCurrent surface analysis places low over southeast Saskatchewan,\nwith high over eastern portions of the Midwest. Upper level\nanalysis places low over central/eastern Montana with persistant\nridge over the upper midwest. Over our area, frontal boundary\ncontinues to push through our area, now making its way into the\nTurtle Mountain and James River Valley regions. Along and ahead\nof the front, showers and a few thunderstorms continue along a\ncorridor of modest instability of about 250-500 J/Kg.\n\nAforementioned convection will continue to lift to the \nnortheast, with some possibly lingering over the Turtle \nMountains through the mid-morning hours. Thereafter, most of us \nwill see a rather quiet day. As we get towards the afternoon and\nevening hours, the next wave lifting ahead of the \naforementioned upper low starts its push into the area as the \nlow actually lifts northward and back a bit to the west, working\na bit into far southwest Saskatchewan. Showers and storm \nchances start in the southwest later this afternoon, then start \nspreading north and easterly overnight, increasing in coverage \nmainly along and west of Highway 83. Late this afternoon into \nthis evening there will be a period where a few storms could \npose a large hail risk over far southwest North Dakota, \ntherefore SPC has posted a Marginal Risk (level 1 of 5) mainly \nover Bowman and Slope Counties. \n\nAs we get into Tuesday, upper low continues to loiter over\nsouthern Saskatchewan with surface low a bit to the east.\nSoutherly flow with this will result in an increase in moisture\nover our area. Showers continue over our area in the morning,\nwith thunderstorm coverage increasing in the afternoon. Model\nguidance has been quite consistent noting the increase in\ninstability over our area with MLCAPE values around 1500-3000 \nJ/Kg in the afternoon/early evening through much of central and \na fair portion of western North Dakota, while 0-6km shear values\npush generally into the 40-50kt range. Therefore, scattered \nsevere storms are expected with a level 2 of 5 risk for severe \nweather. Given the parameters and expected clusters of severe \nstorms, expect the potential for golf ball size hail but would \nnot be shocked to see a storm or two exceed that given the \nstrong shear, along with the potential for 60 mph wind gusts. As\nfor the tornadic threat, there is a period during the late\nafternoon/early evening hours where 0-1 SRH values exceed 100\nm2/s2 over portions of central North Dakota. Therefore, will go\nwith the mention of an isolated tornado possible. As we get\nlater in the evening/overnight severe threat wanes but showers\nand some storms continue.\n\nOn Wednesday upper low meanders easterly through southern\nSaskatchewan into Manitoba, while at the surface cold front\ngradually works its way east through our area. The timing of\nthis cold front will come into play mid-day through the\nafternoon and possibly into the early evening as expect a moist\nunstable atmosphere to again be in place ahead of the front,\nalong with strong deep layer shear (~40-50 kts 0-6km).\nTherefore, there will again be a slight risk (level 2 of 5) for\nsevere weather over the Turtle Mountain region southward into\nthe James River Valley and parts of south central North Dakota,\nbut again the timing of the front will dictate where the severe\nrisk truly ends up that day.  \n\nUpper low then pushes east but we still end up in an active\nzonal pattern bring rain/storm chances through the rest of the \nwork week. Pattern looks to shift more southwest this weekend, \nwith machine learning guidance hinting at increased severe \npotential.",
